WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.919
A Retrieved Reformation, by O. Henry.

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:11.145
A guard came to the prison shoe-shop, where Jimmy Valentine was assiduously stitching uppers, and escorted him to the front office.

00:00:11.145 --> 00:00:16.785
There the warden handed Jimmy his pardon, which had been signed that morning by the governor.

00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:19.530
Jimmy took it in a tired kind of way.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.962
He had served nearly ten months of a four year sentence.

00:00:22.962 --> 00:00:27.270
He had expected to stay only about three months, at the longest.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:36.361
When a man with as many friends on the outside as Jimmy Valentine had is received in the "stir" it is hardly worth while to cut his hair.

00:00:36.361 --> 00:00:40.693
"Now, Valentine," said the warden, "you'll go out in the morning.

00:00:40.693 --> 00:00:43.245
Brace up, and make a man of yourself.

00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:45.374
You're not a bad fellow at heart.

00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:48.191
Stop cracking safes, and live straight."

00:00:48.191 --> 00:00:50.836
"Me?" said Jimmy, in surprise.

00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:53.823
"Why, I never cracked a safe in my life."

00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:56.306
"Oh, no," laughed the warden.

00:00:56.306 --> 00:00:57.504
"Of course not.

00:00:57.504 --> 00:00:58.835
Let's see, now.

00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:02.726
How was it you happened to get sent up on that Springfield job?

00:01:02.726 --> 00:01:09.544
Was it because you wouldn't prove an alibi for fear of compromising somebody in extremely high-toned society?

00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:13.844
Or was it simply a case of a mean old jury that had it in for you?

00:01:13.844 --> 00:01:16.946
It's always one or the other with you innocent victims."

00:01:16.946 --> 00:01:20.106
"Me?" said Jimmy, still blankly virtuous.

00:01:20.106 --> 00:01:24.377
"Why, warden, I never was in Springfield in my life!"

00:01:24.377 --> 00:01:29.854
"Take him back, Cronin!" said the warden, "and fix him up with outgoing clothes.

00:01:29.854 --> 00:01:34.078
Unlock him at seven in the morning, and let him come to the bull-pen.

00:01:34.078 --> 00:01:36.823
Better think over my advice, Valentine."

00:01:36.823 --> 00:01:42.333
At a quarter past seven on the next morning Jimmy stood in the warden's outer office.

00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:52.811
He had on a suit of the villainously fitting, ready-made clothes and a pair of the stiff, squeaky shoes that the state furnishes to its discharged compulsory guests.

00:01:52.811 --> 00:02:02.441
The clerk handed him a railroad ticket and the five-dollar bill with which the law expected him to rehabilitate himself into good citizenship and prosperity.

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:05.579
The warden gave him a cigar, and shook hands.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:12.795
Valentine, 9762, was chronicled on the books, "Pardoned by Governor," and Mr.

00:02:12.795 --> 00:02:16.179
James Valentine walked out into the sunshine.

00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:24.303
Disregarding the song of the birds, the waving green trees, and the smell of the flowers, Jimmy headed straight for a restaurant.

00:02:24.303 --> 00:02:35.536
There he tasted the first sweet joys of liberty in the shape of a broiled chicken and a bottle of white wine—followed by a cigar a grade better than the one the warden had given him.

00:02:35.536 --> 00:02:38.494
From there he proceeded leisurely to the depot.

00:02:38.494 --> 00:02:44.342
He tossed a quarter into the hat of a blind man sitting by the door, and boarded his train.

00:02:44.342 --> 00:02:48.358
Three hours set him down in a little town near the state line.

00:02:48.358 --> 00:02:54.513
He went to the café of one Mike Dolan and shook hands with Mike, who was alone behind the bar.

00:02:54.513 --> 00:02:59.145
"Sorry we couldn't make it sooner, Jimmy, me boy," said Mike.

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:04.801
"But we had that protest from Springfield to buck against, and the governor nearly balked.

00:03:04.801 --> 00:03:06.029
Feeling all right?"

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:07.639
"Fine," said Jimmy.

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:08.665
"Got my key?"

00:03:08.665 --> 00:03:13.958
He got his key and went upstairs, unlocking the door of a room at the rear.

00:03:13.958 --> 00:03:16.322
Everything was just as he had left it.

00:03:16.322 --> 00:03:25.481
There on the floor was still Ben Price's collar-button that had been torn from that eminent detective's shirt-band when they had overpowered Jimmy to arrest him.

00:03:26.096 --> 00:03:33.917
Pulling out from the wall a folding-bed, Jimmy slid back a panel in the wall and dragged out a dust-covered suit-case.

00:03:33.917 --> 00:03:39.441
He opened this and gazed fondly at the finest set of burglar's tools in the East.

00:03:39.441 --> 00:03:55.444
It was a complete set, made of specially tempered steel, the latest designs in drills, punches, braces and bits, jimmies, clamps, and augers, with two or three novelties, invented by Jimmy himself, in which he took pride.

00:03:55.444 --> 00:04:02.364
Over nine hundred dollars they had cost him to have made at ——, a place where they make such things for the profession.

00:04:02.364 --> 00:04:06.479
In half an hour Jimmy went down stairs and through the café.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:13.762
He was now dressed in tasteful and well-fitting clothes, and carried his dusted and cleaned suit-case in his hand.

00:04:13.762 --> 00:04:17.305
"Got anything on?" asked Mike Dolan, genially.

00:04:17.305 --> 00:04:20.089
"Me?" said Jimmy, in a puzzled tone.

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:21.410
"I don't understand.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:27.969
I'm representing the New York Amalgamated Short Snap Biscuit Cracker and Frazzled Wheat Company."

00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:34.538
This statement delighted Mike to such an extent that Jimmy had to take a seltzer-and-milk on the spot.

00:04:34.538 --> 00:04:36.971
He never touched "hard" drinks.

00:04:36.971 --> 00:04:47.896
A week after the release of Valentine, 9762, there was a neat job of safe-burglary done in Richmond, Indiana, with no clue to the author.

00:04:47.896 --> 00:04:51.308
A scant eight hundred dollars was all that was secured.

00:04:51.308 --> 00:05:03.538
Two weeks after that a patented, improved, burglar-proof safe in Logansport was opened like a cheese to the tune of fifteen hundred dollars, currency; securities and silver untouched.

00:05:03.538 --> 00:05:06.181
That began to interest the rogue-catchers.

00:05:06.181 --> 00:05:15.493
Then an old-fashioned bank-safe in Jefferson City became active and threw out of its crater an eruption of bank-notes amounting to five thousand dollars.

00:05:15.493 --> 00:05:21.010
The losses were now high enough to bring the matter up into Ben Price's class of work.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:27.225
By comparing notes, a remarkable similarity in the methods of the burglaries was noticed.

00:05:27.225 --> 00:05:32.419
Ben Price investigated the scenes of the robberies, and was heard to remark:

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:35.359
"That's Dandy Jim Valentine's autograph.

00:05:35.359 --> 00:05:36.956
He's resumed business.

00:05:36.956 --> 00:05:43.034
Look at that combination knob—jerked out as easy as pulling up a radish in wet weather.

00:05:43.034 --> 00:05:45.513
He's got the only clamps that can do it.

00:05:45.513 --> 00:05:48.558
And look how clean those tumblers were punched out!

00:05:48.558 --> 00:05:51.402
Jimmy never has to drill but one hole.

00:05:51.402 --> 00:05:53.093
Yes, I guess I want Mr.

00:05:53.093 --> 00:05:54.214
Valentine.

00:05:54.214 --> 00:05:59.322
He'll do his bit next time without any short-time or clemency foolishness."

00:05:59.322 --> 00:06:01.802
Ben Price knew Jimmy's habits.

00:06:01.802 --> 00:06:04.933
He had learned them while working up the Springfield case.

00:06:04.933 --> 00:06:11.993
Long jumps, quick get-aways, no confederates, and a taste for good society—these ways had helped Mr.

00:06:11.993 --> 00:06:16.725
Valentine to become noted as a successful dodger of retribution.

00:06:16.725 --> 00:06:25.749
It was given out that Ben Price had taken up the trail of the elusive cracksman, and other people with burglar-proof safes felt more at ease.

00:06:25.749 --> 00:06:36.719
One afternoon Jimmy Valentine and his suit-case climbed out of the mail-hack in Elmore, a little town five miles off the railroad down in the black-jack country of Arkansas.

00:06:36.719 --> 00:06:44.318
Jimmy, looking like an athletic young senior just home from college, went down the board side-walk toward the hotel.

00:06:44.318 --> 00:06:51.568
A young lady crossed the street, passed him at the corner and entered a door over which was the sign, "The Elmore Bank."

00:06:51.568 --> 00:06:57.251
Jimmy Valentine looked into her eyes, forgot what he was, and became another man.

00:06:57.251 --> 00:07:00.168
She lowered her eyes and coloured slightly.

00:07:00.168 --> 00:07:04.125
Young men of Jimmy's style and looks were scarce in Elmore.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:15.633
Jimmy collared a boy that was loafing on the steps of the bank as if he were one of the stockholders, and began to ask him questions about the town, feeding him dimes at intervals.

00:07:15.633 --> 00:07:22.968
By and by the young lady came out, looking royally unconscious of the young man with the suit-case, and went her way.

00:07:22.968 --> 00:07:28.338
"Isn't that young lady Polly Simpson?" asked Jimmy, with specious guile.

00:07:28.338 --> 00:07:30.121
"Naw," said the boy.

00:07:30.121 --> 00:07:31.854
"She's Annabel Adams.

00:07:31.854 --> 00:07:33.839
Her pa owns this bank.

00:07:33.839 --> 00:07:35.721
What'd you come to Elmore for?

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:37.572
Is that a gold watch-chain?

00:07:37.572 --> 00:07:39.335
I'm going to get a bulldog.

00:07:39.335 --> 00:07:40.775
Got any more dimes?"

00:07:40.775 --> 00:07:44.413
Jimmy went to the Planters' Hotel, registered as Ralph D.

00:07:44.413 --> 00:07:47.012
Spencer, and engaged a room.

00:07:47.012 --> 00:07:51.090
He leaned on the desk and declared his platform to the clerk.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:55.789
He said he had come to Elmore to look for a location to go into business.

00:07:55.789 --> 00:07:58.755
How was the shoe business, now, in the town?

00:07:58.755 --> 00:08:00.850
He had thought of the shoe business.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:02.037
Was there an opening?

00:08:02.037 --> 00:08:05.499
The clerk was impressed by the clothes and manner of Jimmy.

00:08:05.499 --> 00:08:13.689
He, himself, was something of a pattern of fashion to the thinly gilded youth of Elmore, but he now perceived his shortcomings.

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:19.912
While trying to figure out Jimmy's manner of tying his four-in-hand he cordially gave information.

00:08:19.912 --> 00:08:23.429
Yes, there ought to be a good opening in the shoe line.

00:08:23.429 --> 00:08:26.488
There wasn't an exclusive shoe-store in the place.

00:08:26.488 --> 00:08:29.436
The dry-goods and general stores handled them.

00:08:29.436 --> 00:08:32.163
Business in all lines was fairly good.

00:08:32.163 --> 00:08:32.903
Hoped Mr.

00:08:32.903 --> 00:08:35.837
Spencer would decide to locate in Elmore.

00:08:35.837 --> 00:08:40.705
He would find it a pleasant town to live in, and the people very sociable.

00:08:40.705 --> 00:08:41.090
Mr.

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:46.450
Spencer thought he would stop over in the town a few days and look over the situation.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.115
No, the clerk needn't call the boy.

00:08:49.115 --> 00:08:53.571
He would carry up his suit-case, himself; it was rather heavy.

00:08:53.571 --> 00:08:53.991
Mr.

00:08:53.991 --> 00:09:05.903
Ralph Spencer, the phoenix that arose from Jimmy Valentine's ashes—ashes left by the flame of a sudden and alterative attack of love—remained in Elmore, and prospered.

00:09:05.903 --> 00:09:09.809
He opened a shoe-store and secured a good run of trade.

00:09:09.809 --> 00:09:13.789
Socially he was also a success, and made many friends.

00:09:13.789 --> 00:09:16.252
And he accomplished the wish of his heart.

00:09:16.252 --> 00:09:21.836
He met Miss Annabel Adams, and became more and more captivated by her charms.

00:09:21.836 --> 00:09:24.238
At the end of a year the situation of Mr.

00:09:24.238 --> 00:09:34.395
Ralph Spencer was this: he had won the respect of the community, his shoe-store was flourishing, and he and Annabel were engaged to be married in two weeks.

00:09:34.395 --> 00:09:34.830
Mr.

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:40.080
Adams, the typical, plodding, country banker, approved of Spencer.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.622
Annabel's pride in him almost equalled her affection.

00:09:43.622 --> 00:09:46.133
He was as much at home in the family of Mr.

00:09:46.133 --> 00:09:50.934
Adams and that of Annabel's married sister as if he were already a member.

00:09:50.934 --> 00:09:57.709
One day Jimmy sat down in his room and wrote this letter, which he mailed to the safe address of one of his old friends in St.

00:09:57.709 --> 00:09:59.058
Louis:

00:09:59.058 --> 00:10:00.445
Dear OLD PAL:

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:07.042
I want you to be at Sullivan's place, in Little Rock, next Wednesday night, at nine o'clock.

00:10:07.042 --> 00:10:09.761
I want you to wind up some little matters for me.

00:10:09.761 --> 00:10:14.048
And, also, I want to make you a present of my kit of tools.

00:10:14.048 --> 00:10:19.459
I know you'll be glad to get them—you couldn't duplicate the lot for a thousand dollars.

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:23.872
Say, Billy, I've quit the old business—a year ago.

00:10:23.872 --> 00:10:25.530
I've got a nice store.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:31.430
I'm making an honest living, and I'm going to marry the finest girl on earth two weeks from now.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.369
It's the only life, Billy—the straight one.

00:10:34.369 --> 00:10:38.421
I wouldn't touch a dollar of another man's money now for a million.

00:10:38.421 --> 00:10:46.367
After I get married I'm going to sell out and go West, where there won't be so much danger of having old scores brought up against me.

00:10:46.367 --> 00:10:49.042
I tell you, Billy, she's an angel.

00:10:49.042 --> 00:10:53.844
She believes in me; and I wouldn't do another crooked thing for the whole world.

00:10:53.844 --> 00:10:57.070
Be sure to be at Sully's, for I must see you.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.114
I'll bring along the tools with me.

00:10:59.114 --> 00:11:00.315
Your old friend,

00:11:00.315 --> 00:11:01.099
JIMMY.

00:11:01.099 --> 00:11:08.948
On the Monday night after Jimmy wrote this letter, Ben Price jogged unobtrusively into Elmore in a livery buggy.

00:11:08.948 --> 00:11:14.117
He lounged about town in his quiet way until he found out what he wanted to know.

00:11:14.117 --> 00:11:18.966
From the drug-store across the street from Spencer's shoe-store he got a good look at Ralph D.

00:11:18.966 --> 00:11:20.432
Spencer.

00:11:20.432 --> 00:11:26.201
"Going to marry the banker's daughter are you, Jimmy?" said Ben to himself, softly.

00:11:26.201 --> 00:11:27.842
"Well, I don't know!"

00:11:27.842 --> 00:11:31.049
The next morning Jimmy took breakfast at the Adamses.

00:11:31.049 --> 00:11:36.888
He was going to Little Rock that day to order his wedding-suit and buy something nice for Annabel.

00:11:36.888 --> 00:11:41.253
That would be the first time he had left town since he came to Elmore.

00:11:41.253 --> 00:11:48.144
It had been more than a year now since those last professional "jobs," and he thought he could safely venture out.

00:11:48.144 --> 00:11:52.792
After breakfast quite a family party went downtown together—Mr.

00:11:52.792 --> 00:12:00.176
Adams, Annabel, Jimmy, and Annabel's married sister with her two little girls, aged five and nine.

00:12:00.176 --> 00:12:06.645
They came by the hotel where Jimmy still boarded, and he ran up to his room and brought along his suit-case.

00:12:06.645 --> 00:12:08.427
Then they went on to the bank.

00:12:08.427 --> 00:12:14.963
There stood Jimmy's horse and buggy and Dolph Gibson, who was going to drive him over to the railroad station.

00:12:14.963 --> 00:12:20.991
All went inside the high, carved oak railings into the banking-room—Jimmy included, for Mr.

00:12:20.991 --> 00:12:24.673
Adams's future son-in-law was welcome anywhere.

00:12:24.673 --> 00:12:31.699
The clerks were pleased to be greeted by the good-looking, agreeable young man who was going to marry Miss Annabel.

00:12:31.699 --> 00:12:34.029
Jimmy set his suit-case down.

00:12:34.029 --> 00:12:41.575
Annabel, whose heart was bubbling with happiness and lively youth, put on Jimmy's hat, and picked up the suit-case.

00:12:41.575 --> 00:12:44.609
"Wouldn't I make a nice drummer?" said Annabel.

00:12:44.609 --> 00:12:45.211
"My!

00:12:45.211 --> 00:12:47.440
Ralph, how heavy it is?

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.027
Feels like it was full of gold bricks."

00:12:50.027 --> 00:12:56.066
"Lot of nickel-plated shoe-horns in there," said Jimmy, coolly, "that I'm going to return.

00:12:56.066 --> 00:12:59.218
Thought I'd save express charges by taking them up.

00:12:59.218 --> 00:13:01.214
I'm getting awfully economical."

00:13:01.829 --> 00:13:05.721
The Elmore Bank had just put in a new safe and vault.

00:13:05.721 --> 00:13:06.172
Mr.

00:13:06.172 --> 00:13:11.317
Adams was very proud of it, and insisted on an inspection by every one.

00:13:11.317 --> 00:13:15.788
The vault was a small one, but it had a new, patented door.

00:13:15.788 --> 00:13:23.033
It fastened with three solid steel bolts thrown simultaneously with a single handle, and had a time-lock.

00:13:23.033 --> 00:13:23.504
Mr.

00:13:23.504 --> 00:13:26.245
Adams beamingly explained its workings to Mr.

00:13:26.245 --> 00:13:30.956
Spencer, who showed a courteous but not too intelligent interest.

00:13:30.956 --> 00:13:37.543
The two children, May and Agatha, were delighted by the shining metal and funny clock and knobs.

00:13:37.543 --> 00:13:45.140
While they were thus engaged Ben Price sauntered in and leaned on his elbow, looking casually inside between the railings.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:50.527
He told the teller that he didn't want anything; he was just waiting for a man he knew.

00:13:50.527 --> 00:13:54.853
Suddenly there was a scream or two from the women, and a commotion.

00:13:54.853 --> 00:14:02.269
Unperceived by the elders, May, the nine-year-old girl, in a spirit of play, had shut Agatha in the vault.

00:14:02.269 --> 00:14:06.929
She had then shot the bolts and turned the knob of the combination as she had seen Mr.

00:14:06.929 --> 00:14:08.573
Adams do.

00:14:08.573 --> 00:14:12.930
The old banker sprang to the handle and tugged at it for a moment.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.354
"The door can't be opened," he groaned.

00:14:15.354 --> 00:14:18.853
"The clock hasn't been wound nor the combination set."

00:14:18.853 --> 00:14:22.202
Agatha's mother screamed again, hysterically.

00:14:22.202 --> 00:14:23.669
"Hush!" said Mr.

00:14:23.669 --> 00:14:26.280
Adams, raising his trembling hand.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.198
"All be quite for a moment.

00:14:28.198 --> 00:14:31.300
Agatha!" he called as loudly as he could.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:32.439
"Listen to me."

00:14:32.439 --> 00:14:40.714
During the following silence they could just hear the faint sound of the child wildly shrieking in the dark vault in a panic of terror.

00:14:40.714 --> 00:14:43.411
"My precious darling!" wailed the mother.

00:14:43.411 --> 00:14:45.157
"She will die of fright!

00:14:45.157 --> 00:14:46.290
Open the door!

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:47.944
Oh, break it open!

00:14:47.944 --> 00:14:49.591
Can't you men do something?"

00:14:49.591 --> 00:14:53.572
"There isn't a man nearer than Little Rock who can open that door," said Mr.

00:14:53.572 --> 00:14:56.150
Adams, in a shaky voice.

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:57.105
"My God!

00:14:57.105 --> 00:14:59.298
Spencer, what shall we do?

00:14:59.298 --> 00:15:02.533
That child—she can't stand it long in there.

00:15:02.533 --> 00:15:07.789
There isn't enough air, and, besides, she'll go into convulsions from fright."

00:15:07.789 --> 00:15:12.747
Agatha's mother, frantic now, beat the door of the vault with her hands.

00:15:12.747 --> 00:15:15.400
Somebody wildly suggested dynamite.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:21.175
Annabel turned to Jimmy, her large eyes full of anguish, but not yet despairing.

00:15:21.175 --> 00:15:26.472
To a woman nothing seems quite impossible to the powers of the man she worships.

00:15:26.472 --> 00:15:29.799
"Can't you do something, Ralph—try, won't you?"

00:15:29.799 --> 00:15:35.065
He looked at her with a queer, soft smile on his lips and in his keen eyes.

00:15:35.065 --> 00:15:39.522
"Annabel," he said, "give me that rose you are wearing, will you?"

00:15:39.522 --> 00:15:46.904
Hardly believing that she heard him aright, she unpinned the bud from the bosom of her dress, and placed it in his hand.

00:15:46.904 --> 00:15:52.748
Jimmy stuffed it into his vest-pocket, threw off his coat and pulled up his shirt-sleeves.

00:15:52.748 --> 00:15:54.031
With that act Ralph D.

00:15:54.031 --> 00:15:58.294
Spencer passed away and Jimmy Valentine took his place.

00:15:58.294 --> 00:16:02.841
"Get away from the door, all of you," he commanded, shortly.

00:16:02.841 --> 00:16:06.932
He set his suit-case on the table, and opened it out flat.

00:16:06.932 --> 00:16:11.906
From that time on he seemed to be unconscious of the presence of any one else.

00:16:11.906 --> 00:16:20.227
He laid out the shining, queer implements swiftly and orderly, whistling softly to himself as he always did when at work.

00:16:20.227 --> 00:16:25.359
In a deep silence and immovable, the others watched him as if under a spell.

00:16:25.974 --> 00:16:30.832
In a minute Jimmy's pet drill was biting smoothly into the steel door.

00:16:30.832 --> 00:16:37.268
In ten minutes—breaking his own burglarious record—he threw back the bolts and opened the door.

00:16:37.268 --> 00:16:42.900
Agatha, almost collapsed, but safe, was gathered into her mother's arms.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:48.752
Jimmy Valentine put on his coat, and walked outside the railings towards the front door.

00:16:48.752 --> 00:16:53.944
As he went he thought he heard a far-away voice that he once knew call "Ralph!"

00:16:53.944 --> 00:16:55.556
But he never hesitated.

00:16:55.556 --> 00:16:58.783
At the door a big man stood somewhat in his way.

00:16:58.783 --> 00:17:03.640
"Hello, Ben!" said Jimmy, still with his strange smile.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:05.755
"Got around at last, have you?

00:17:05.755 --> 00:17:07.096
Well, let's go.

00:17:07.096 --> 00:17:09.811
I don't know that it makes much difference, now."

00:17:09.811 --> 00:17:12.813
And then Ben Price acted rather strangely.

00:17:12.813 --> 00:17:14.427
"Guess you're mistaken, Mr.

00:17:14.427 --> 00:17:16.065
Spencer," he said.

00:17:16.065 --> 00:17:18.068
"Don't believe I recognize you.

00:17:18.068 --> 00:17:20.340
Your buggy's waiting for you, ain't it?"

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:23.422
And Ben Price turned and strolled down the street.
